     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Acetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6 g/day, which is well above the usual adult maximum of 4 g/day. Many institutions cap it at 3 g/day. A single 2000 mg dose is also double the standard maximum of 1000 mg. At this dose the risk of acetaminophen hepatotoxicity is significant, even with normal liver function and a weight of 70 kg.\n\nHere are some options:\n\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily**, which is 3 g/day.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily**, which is 4 g/day and the usual maximum. It could be given every 6 hours.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours**, which is 2.6 g/day.\n\nIf you meant a different dose, such as 1000 mg or 200 mg, tell me the correct dose and I'll enter it right away. If you do want 2000 mg three times daily, please confirm that it's intentional and give the clinical rationale, so I can add it to the order note. Your institution's high-alert or dose-limit policies may also require pharmacy review."
      }
     ],
